\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Internet}}{1}{section*.1}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{.}}{1}{section*.2}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{emphasizing the power of stance in policing what emotions and positions are ``allowed'' and drawing boundaries between ``us'' and ``them''.}}{1}{section*.3}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In partisan meme wars, the creative production and intertextuality of memes largely operate on the levels of content and form, while stance functions as the crucial ``switch'' that tells in-group audiences how to feel and where the line between ``us'' and ``them'' should be drawn.}}{1}{section*.4}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Compared with other online symbols such as straightforward text posts or static campaign logos, Internet memes are thus distinctive in how efficiently they carry stance. Their humor, remixability, and dense intertextuality make them attractive to platform algorithms that reward engagement, while their playful ambiguity offers plausible deniability (``it's just a joke'') even when they carry strong biased messages \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {yoon2016notjustajoke}. Sharing a meme becomes a low-cost identity performance: by reposting a familiar template or clip, users publicly signal which side they belong to and which side they ridicule.}}{2}{section*.5}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Another highly visible Republican meme object was Trump's August 2023 mug shot from Fulton County, as journalism \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {yang2023mugshottomugs} and early meme studies \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {meleshchenko2024multimodalmetaphtonymy} documented how the image was instantly remixed into countless reaction memes, then appeared on T-shirts, mugs, and donation links, and eventually was reframed as proof that Trump is a persecuted yet defiant champion of ``the people''.}}{2}{section*.6}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Rather than replacing traditional campaign communication, these partisan meme streams now run alongside official messaging, offering highly shareable visual narratives that help each side define its heroes, villains, and version of what is at stake in the election.}}{2}{section*.7}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{However, }}{2}{section*.8}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{these}}{2}{section*.9}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In this way, shifts in stance do not just change the surface meaning of a meme; they restructure the social relationships that the meme organizes, intensifying the ``us''/``them'' dynamic among online community.}}{3}{section*.10}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{politainment, a portmanteau word composed of politics and entertainment proposed by \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\textcite {doerner2001politainment} more than twenty years ago, has entered a new era of intensified online meme warfare.}}{3}{section*.11}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{intensified partisan meme warfare is increasingly blurring the line between entertainment and political participation.}}{3}{section*.12}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(F): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Still, it is important not to overstate the negative impact of partisan meme wars. Memes do not operate in a vacuum: they appear embedded in captions, comment sections, and recommendation feeds that shape how audiences interpret them. The same coconut tree clip, for instance, might be posted in an explicitly political thread with a caption attacking Harris, but it might also circulate in more casual ``cringe compilation'' or fan-edit contexts where the focus is on entertainment rather than electoral choice. Young users can encounter the same image macro as a serious political statement in one context and as a silly in-joke in another.}}{4}{section*.13}%
\contentsline {added}{Added~(F): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Moreover, the young people most active in producing and sharing partisan memes may already be highly engaged and strongly identified with a party. For them, meme warfare probably does not flip their loyalties; instead, it solidifies pre-existing identities, giving them emotionally satisfying ways to celebrate ``us'' and condemn ``them'' \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\parencite {galipeau2023impactpoliticalmemes}. A teenager who occasionally encounters a Trump mug shot meme in a non-political group chat might chuckle and scroll on, while a committed partisan in a politicized TikTok bubble may interpret the same image as a rallying symbol or an unforgivable insult. From this perspective, partisan memes might function less as tools of mass persuasion and more as maintenance mechanisms for already polarized communities. They help loyal supporters feel seen and connected, but their ability to change minds or mobilize large numbers of previously disengaged young citizens is less clear. Paying attention to the contexts in which memes appear---their captions, comment threads, and the composition of the audiences that share them---thus helps to nuance claims about their impact. The negative effects that this paper has emphasized are most likely to be pronounced in dense, partisan online subcultures rather than evenly distributed across all young social media users.}}{4}{section*.14}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{and revision suggestions}}{5}{section*.15}%
